You are analyzing mathematical statements extracted from a single paper.
Your task is to find non-obvious dependencies between statements — ones that a
simple LaTeX cross-reference scanner would miss. Bare \ref/\eqref/\cref commands
in a statement's body or proof are already captured elsewhere; do not repeat them.

**What to look for:**

In body, note, or proof — natural language only (no reference commands):
  Dependencies expressed through logic or narrative without a \ref command, e.g.:
  "which follows from the above", "by the same argument as in the previous lemma",
  "combining with the bound established earlier", "the notation introduced above".
  Skip any dependency that is solely expressed via a \ref/\eqref/\cref/\autoref/
  \Cref/\hyperref command — those are handled separately.

In pre_context or post_context — two kinds:
  (1) Reference commands (\ref etc.) that appear together with explicit location-based
      words ("above", "below", "preceding", "following", "previous", "next") or
      logic-based words ("it follows", "as a consequence", "combining", "applying").
      A lone \ref without such context does NOT qualify.
  (2) Pure natural language: location- or logic-based references between adjacent
      statements, even without any \ref command
      (e.g. "As a direct consequence of the above theorem", "The following lemma
      strengthens the preceding result").

Each statement has an "id" field and may have the following text fields:
- body: the statement itself
- note: optional bracketed annotation (e.g. theorem name/qualifier)
- proof: the proof of the statement
- pre_context: text immediately before the statement in the source
- post_context: text immediately after the statement in the source

For each dependency you find, output all four of the following fields — all are required:
- "src": the id of the statement that references another
- "dep": the id of the statement being referenced
- "location": which field of src contains the reference — one of:
  body, note, proof, pre_context, post_context
- "phrase": verbatim evidence from that field.
  • For body, note, or proof: shortest verbatim excerpt, typically 3–8 words
    (e.g. "by the same argument as above").
  • For pre_context or post_context: a full clause of 6–15 words verbatim — never
    a bare \ref or theorem name alone. Must include the location/logic anchor words.
    BAD:  "\ref{thm:main}"
    BAD:  "Theorem 1.1"
    GOOD: "As a direct consequence of the above theorem, we obtain"
    GOOD: "The following result follows from \Cref{thm:main} combined with"
    If evidence is spread across disconnected fragments, join with " | ".
  Never paraphrase — all text in "phrase" must appear verbatim in the source field.

Respond with valid YAML only, in this exact format:
dependencies:
- src: "<id>"
  dep: "<id>"
  location: "<location>"
  phrase: "<exact phrase>"

If no dependencies are found, respond with:
dependencies: []
